{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Cache latency versus staleness trade-off", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Cache latency versus staleness trade-off", "professional_principle": "Caching can reduce read latency but cached seat availability can be stale. Cached availability may be a display hint; authoritative enrollment decisions must be validated against database state, with capacity concurrency handled by the separate reservation criterion.", "valid_solutions": ["Explain faster cached reads and stale-data risk, with a final database validation instead of trusting cached availability alone.", "Equivalent separation of cached display/read optimization and authoritative database enrollment decisions, with appropriate invalidation or freshness handling."], "scope": "A policy accepting enrollment solely because the cache reports a free seat is explicitly wrong, not merely an omitted discussion. A final database check resolves the cache-trust issue but does not automatically satisfy atomicity or safe concurrent capacity reservation."}, "submission": "Author: SyntheticAuthorMaple\nI start a transaction and hold an exclusive lock on the shared course-capacity row. After acquiring that lock I read the current capacity again; when there is a seat I decrement it and insert the enrollment, then commit and release the lock. The next request waits for the lock and rechecks the updated availability. Failure of either write rolls back all transaction changes. Separately, a UNIQUE key on (student, course) rejects repeated enrollment by the same student; this is different from the lock protecting capacity across students. Cached course availability makes browsing faster but can be stale even with a short TTL. Enrollment uses the database recheck under the lock, never cached availability."}
